

KLOG(4)              UNIX Programmer's Manual               KLOG(4)



NAME
     klog - kernel log ring, as a readable device

SYNOPSIS
     fd = open("/dev/klog", O_RDONLY);

DESCRIPTION
     /dev/klog is a read-only view of the kernel's message ring
     with a position of its own.  Each read returns the bytes
     written since the last one, and a read on a caught-up
     descriptor waits until there is something rather than
     reporting end of file.  It is what syslogd(8) reads kernel
     messages from, so that they land in the same log as the
     messages syslog(3) senders write to /dev/log.

     ONE READER.  A second open is refused EBUSY.  Two readers
     would share the one position, so each would see an arbitrary
     part of the log and neither would see all of it.

     The position is set once, when the module loads, at the
     oldest message the ring holds, so the first reader gets the
     boot log.  A reopen -- a restarted syslogd(8) -- resumes
     where the last reader stopped instead of reading it all
     again.  If the ring had wrapped when the module loaded, the
     position starts caught up; dmesg(1) still has the history.

     FIONBIO is honoured, so a caught-up read can return EAGAIN
     instead of waiting.  select(2) reports the device readable
     exactly when the reader has not caught up, which is how
     syslogd watches it alongside its socket.

     dmesg(1) IS UNAFFECTED.  It copies the whole ring out and
     consumes nothing, so it and /dev/klog are independent
     readers with independent positions, and reading one does not
     take bytes from the other.

SEE ALSO
     dmesg(1), syslog(3), unix(4)

BUGS
     A WOKEN READER CAN WAIT UP TO A SECOND.  Upstream releases a
     blocked reader when the message is written; this one sleeps
     on the channel the kernel wakes once a second, so a message
     can sit in the ring that long before the reader sees it.  No
     message is lost and the order is not disturbed, but a kernel
     message and a user message written in the same instant can
     reach the log a second apart, and a message written just
     before the machine stops may not be read at all.  The reason
     is a budget: the wakeup belongs in the ring writer, which is
     core-bank code with five bytes free.  apple/fgx#461.

     A READER THAT FALLS A WHOLE RING BEHIND LOSES DATA
     SILENTLY.  The writer does not consult readers -- it cannot
     afford to, for the same reason as above -- and the ring
     carries no generation count, only a write index and a
     wrapped flag.  So a reader the writer laps sees the newest
     bytes, in order, with no indication that anything was
     skipped.  Upstream counts that in its overrun field because
     upstream's writer is the one that discards.  It takes four
     kilobytes of kernel messages inside one wakeup period.

     THE EINTR PATH WAS BROKEN, NOT MERELY UNCOVERED.  A read
     waiting on a caught-up ring returns EINTR when a signal
     arrives, and that arm is written the way pty(4)'s is -- but
     the sleeper was never marked blocked, so neither a signal
     nor the clock could reach it and the read never returned.
     An earlier probe read that as "alarm(2) does not interrupt
     a sleeping task"; it was this defect, apple/fgx#468, and it
     is fixed.  The klogintr test drives the arm with ^C on a
     real blocking read.

     THE KERNEL'S TRACE LINES ARE NOT DELIVERED.  The ring also
     carries the scheduler's own instrumentation -- a line per
     kernel-slot and stack move -- which upstream's message
     buffer never held, and the notes the kernel keeps off the
     console: a refused fork, a refused heap grow, a console
     switch.  Each such line opens with a marker byte, and this
     device steps over it whole, so a reader is neither handed
     nor woken by it; dmesg(8) still shows the whole ring.
     Delivered, the instrumentation reached syslogd(8) about once
     a second and kept the root file system busy, and the notes
     reached root's terminal as kern.crit.  apple/fgx#482.

     Upstream's FIOASYNC, FIONREAD and TIOCSPGRP are not
     implemented.  syslogd(8) uses none of them: it opens the
     device read-only, puts it in a select(2) mask and reads it.

HISTORY
     /dev/klog appeared in 4.2BSD.
